n reach **8**, indexing one row *past the end* of all three `[8][...]` tables — an out-of-bounds read in the original C. I found this is not stable even between two C builds: gcc `-O2` emits the tables in *reverse* declaration order, while gcc `-O0` and clang use declaration order with 8 bytes of zero padding after `g_scf_long`. So the C reference itself disagrees with itself here.\n\nSince `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` (i.e. `-O0`), I mirrored **that** layout exactly in one `#[repr(C)]` blob, with compile-time `offset_of!` assertions locking it in. This makes `long[8]` and `short[8]` byte-identical to the reference. Only `mixed[8]` (200 of 18,580 OOB cases — needs `block_type==2` *and* `mixed_block_flag` *and* `sr_idx==8`) still differs, because there the C reads into `.eh_frame_hdr` unwind data whose bytes are PC-relative offsets that no two builds agree on. My version returns deterministic zeros instead of faulting. Even counting those, strict-mode divergence is 903/1,000,000 (0.09%), confined entirely to that unreproducible-by-construction case."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":494,"cache_read_input_tokens":81235,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":494,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d1d9535-4689-4dbb-a7fc-d25181a2e752","uuid":"70478db3-470d-419e-ba23-0855ab497704","timestamp":"2026-08-19T10:40:18.107Z"}
